{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-pdb015","ts":"2026-05-13T19:30:00Z","title":"population_density_bucket","hypothesis":"Population varies over four orders of magnitude across block groups; a single linear coefficient cannot express the difference between rural (Population < 500), suburban (~2000), urban (~10000), and dense urban (>20000) tiers. Bucketing Population into 4-5 density bins captures non-linear price effects. Distinct from house_age_bucket (different target column) and from kmeans_location_cluster (Population alone rather than spatial coordinates).","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"explore_axis:categorical_bucket_population","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"population_density_bucket","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.4,"estimated_implement_minutes":15,"content_hash":"8e7d9ea29df36d9f283e76639ec7a4093020fc64b030948a8fa7d88cdcc9305d","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.65,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: medium-high novelty — extends categorical-bucketing axis to Population (only house_age_bucket covered bucketing previously). Medium signal: Population-derived features are the one empirically-supported family in this project (run_05 population_per_household +0.014). Cheap.","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-wh008","ts":"2026-05-13T17:00:00Z","title":"winsorize_heavy_tails","hypothesis":"AveRooms, AveBedrms, AveOccup, and Population have heavy right tails with extreme outliers (e.g., AveRooms > 100, AveOccup > 1000 in a few rows) that wreck OLS by dragging the fit. Winsorizing these columns at the 99th percentile (or capping to a fixed threshold) should improve baseline R² with no new feature semantics — pure preprocessing orthogonal to ratio/log/interaction families.","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"explore_axis:preprocessing","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"winsorize_heavy_tails","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.4,"estimated_implement_minutes":15,"content_hash":"0f9da3d90fee4a17af7a42a3b43cbe75a8e6512605739f32e8f5d2cbf660217d","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.82,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: top score under equal novelty+signal weighting. Highest novelty — only outlier-capping preprocessing axis in queue. Strong signal: AveOccup/AveRooms outliers are documented OLS killers in this dataset. Cheap. (Was 0.80 in iter 5 EXPLOIT; mild boost under EXPLORE due to novelty premium.)","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-cd007","ts":"2026-05-13T17:00:00Z","title":"coastal_distance","hypothesis":"California's coastal premium is large but not captured by raw Latitude or Longitude alone. A derived feature approximating distance-to-coast (e.g., Longitude shifted relative to the coastal meridian ~ -124.0 W, or a piecewise distance taking Latitude into account for the coastal arc) should explain a large fraction of price variance unexplained by raw coordinates.","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"explore_axis:geographic_derivation","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"coastal_distance","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.5,"estimated_implement_minutes":20,"content_hash":"6329b5e27b5e8073724fe07c837bd2d0891c80946e8cc996cfd0475fc8b9e2d9","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.80,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: high novelty (geographic-derivation axis distinct from polynomial lat×lon and from k-means clusters) + high signal (CA coastal premium is a top-3 real driver of median_house_value). Boosted vs iter 5 EXPLOIT 0.74 because EXPLORE rewards distinct axes more.","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-ipr011","ts":"2026-05-13T18:30:00Z","title":"income_per_room","hypothesis":"MedInc / AveRooms captures purchasing power per unit of dwelling size (affordability density). Distinct from MedInc alone (no size context), log_median_income (univariate transform), rooms_per_household (no income), and bedrooms_per_room (no income). Cross-axis ratio that fuses the income axis and the density axis — neither single-axis transform can express this dollar-per-room dimension.","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"explore_axis:cross_axis_ratio","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"income_per_room","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.4,"estimated_implement_minutes":15,"content_hash":"9b9b4962d544d7fa6fabec47ae7d74dbc19bf642d6fab8da123c420c3a1ccebf","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.74,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: demoted from iter 5 EXPLOIT 0.88. Signal remains strong (only proven family pattern from run_05), but novelty is moderate — queue has 4 prior ratio features already explored. EXPLORE-phase equal weighting on novelty levels this back to the high-T1 band rather than top.","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-klc010","ts":"2026-05-13T18:00:00Z","title":"kmeans_location_cluster","hypothesis":"Cluster (Latitude, Longitude) into ~10 spatial regions via k-means and use one-hot cluster_id indicators as features. Unlike lat_long_interaction (smooth polynomial surface) and coastal_distance (1D derived), k-means captures sharp regional discontinuities — Bay Area, LA Basin, San Diego, Central Valley, Sierra — that a single global linear/polynomial surface cannot represent. Opens an unsupervised-derived-feature axis the queue does not cover.","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"explore_axis:unsupervised_clustering","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"kmeans_location_cluster","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.5,"estimated_implement_minutes":20,"content_hash":"93a9fba02ab1109e11f04db83172340b02714ff79ea5af471aaa1904559fbe9f","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.72,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: boosted vs iter 5 EXPLOIT 0.68. Only unsupervised-feature axis in queue (very high novelty); signal moderate (k=10 clusters expressive but expensive vs simpler geographic features).","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-mxh012","ts":"2026-05-13T18:30:00Z","title":"medinc_x_houseage","hypothesis":"MedInc * HouseAge multiplicative interaction. High-income established neighborhoods with old housing stock (Berkeley, Pasadena) command different premiums than high-income new construction (Irvine). A multiplicative term lets the income coefficient vary with housing-stock age — a cross-axis interaction the queue does not cover (house_age_bucket is age-only, medinc_quadratic is income-only).","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"explore_axis:non_geo_interaction","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"medinc_x_houseage","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.3,"estimated_implement_minutes":10,"content_hash":"ba0722a51446ac4b880f1e4d194208dfc0ca3a59269ce1444ff23b43a8c640b9","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.70,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: boosted vs iter 5 EXPLOIT 0.64. Only non-geographic interaction in queue; high novelty. Medium signal (interactions are usually small effects). Cheap.","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-lli005","ts":"2026-05-13T16:00:00Z","title":"lat_long_interaction","hypothesis":"Latitude and Longitude individually map weakly to median_house_value, but their interaction (Latitude * Longitude, plus quadratic terms Lat^2 and Lon^2) captures California's diagonal coastal-to-inland price gradient that neither raw coordinate explains alone.","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"GOAL.md:obvious_wins","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"lat_long_interaction","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.5,"estimated_implement_minutes":20,"content_hash":"7b94b508ae610abbb3403905959ea0ce90a18449f91ecd4221f1caa6a03fe8f1","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.64,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: demoted from 0.70. Under EXPLORE-phase equal weighting, polynomial lat×lon is partly redundant with coastal_distance (1D derived) and kmeans_location_cluster (sharp discontinuities) — novelty is reduced because two stronger geographic ideas are already in the queue. Still T1 (GOAL.md listed it).","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-hab006","ts":"2026-05-13T16:00:00Z","title":"house_age_bucket","hypothesis":"HouseAge has a non-linear relationship with median_house_value: very old neighborhoods (>40y) and very new developments command different premiums than middle-aged stock. Ordinal bucketing into 4-5 age bins should outperform a single linear coefficient.","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"GOAL.md:obvious_wins","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"house_age_bucket","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.4,"estimated_implement_minutes":15,"content_hash":"dfd12f142835969eabc5eadb1ec14d1dc44e9b2c4da9998c0d8aefea48d5c0b3","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T1","tier_score":0.62,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: slight boost vs iter 5 0.60. Categorical-bucketing axis (HouseAge) — moderate novelty (this iter introduced population_density_bucket which is a sibling). Medium signal (non-linear age effects real but moderate magnitude).","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
{"schema":"brainstorm/v1","id":"BS-2026-05-13-mq009","ts":"2026-05-13T17:00:00Z","title":"medinc_quadratic","hypothesis":"Income-to-price has diminishing returns at the high end and a steeper slope in the mid-range. Adding MedInc^2 alongside MedInc captures the concave/luxury bend that a linear coefficient misses — distinct from log_median_income because polynomial expansion can be U-shaped and is symmetric in interpretation rather than monotone-concave.","source":{"kind":"planner","ref":"explore_axis:polynomial_transform","finding_id":null},"feature_family_name_hint":"medinc_quadratic","row_grain":"(row_id)","estimated_cost_dollars":0.3,"estimated_implement_minutes":10,"content_hash":"e1c6293517e2aba2758bec3752b78cabc90b0c299817fde2fb558e290a99ef0b","embedding_hash":null,"embedding_uri":null,"tier":"T2","tier_score":0.52,"tier_rationale":"EXPLORE iter 6: hold T2. Same input column as failed log_median_income (run_03 -0.202). Low novelty (polynomial is a sibling of log, both univariate income transforms). Eligible under EXPLORE (T0/T1/T2) but ranked lowest.","status":"queued","iteration_id":null,"run_id":null,"iteration_history":[]}
